WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:01.380
SPEAKER: Hi, I'm [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.150
And today, I'm super
excited to talk

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:06.630
about securing your APIs using
Google Cloud's Apigee and Cloud

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.560
Armor.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.490
Let's get to it.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.890
So why are APIs so great?

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.470
Well, APIs significantly
reduce the time

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.560
to market of new
innovations and experiences

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.290
by providing a simple and
easy way for developers

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.570
to consume data
and functionality

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.270
that resides in your
backend systems.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:36.870
Because APIs are packages of
business critical-information,

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.020
they're also targets
for malicious attacks.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.340
Google Cloud Apigee also
saw over 170% increase

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.210
in abusive API traffic
in the past year.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.420
To help you protect APIs
against security threats,

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.870
Google Cloud brings together
Apigee and Cloud Armor,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.020
the best of API management
and web application firewall

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:59.870
solutions.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:04.140
While Apigee offers OAuth, API
keys and role-based access,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.230
and many other API-level
security features,

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:11.640
Cloud Armor offers network-level
security such as DDoS

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.580
protection, geo-fencing,
and mitigation

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.280
of OWASP top 10 risks.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.310
Let's see a quick demo
of how this works.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.410
For the purpose of this
demo, I have created

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.470
a Hello, World API in Apigee.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.370
To protect this API, let's first
start by adding a verify API

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.610
key policy to this proxy.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.620
Once we do that,
you'll see the default

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.410
is set to a query param.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.920
Let's change this to a header.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.125
I'm now going to save
it as a new revision.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:49.550
And deploy it to the
demo environment.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.030
To consume this API, let's
create an API product

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:57.110
and add our API proxy to this
product by specifying the path.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:01:58.880
With this, the
Hello, World proxy

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.130
is now ready and accessible.

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.740
When I try to access
this API proxy,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.940
Apigee would block it since
we didn't pass an API key.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:10.580
Malicious clients could
still send a lot of requests

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.250
and interrupt traffic
processing by the gateway

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:15.920
for legitimate users.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:18.110
So to further
protect this API, I

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.140
created a very basic
Google in Cloud Armor

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.630
for the simplicity of this demo.

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:26.360
This rule checks for
the API key header,

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.130
though it doesn't validate
the actual key itself.

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.020
I'm now going to apply this rule
to the Apigee load balancer.

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:38.400
And apply this
policy to the target.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.830
Now, when I go to the load
balancing section under Network

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:56.350
services, and look into the
details of the load balancer,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:00.460
I see that the security policy
has Cloud Armor attached to it.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.260
Let's now try once
again to access

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:06.940
the same Hello, World API
without passing any API key.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:09.400
You'll notice that the error
this time is at a network

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.560
level from Cloud Armor.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:15.460
If the same API is accessed
with a header and an invalid API

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:18.070
key, then Cloud Armor
is going to clear it.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.200
The Apigee would do
further validation

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.140
and block it due
to faulty API key.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.970
That was just a
preview of how to add

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:27.220
multi-layer security
for your APIs

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:29.740
with Apigee and Cloud Armor.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:33.040
In summary, Apigee and
Cloud Armor offer integrated

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.470
out-of-the-box box security
capabilities to protect

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.750
your APIs at multiple levels.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.850
Now it's your turn
to try it out.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:42.760
And get started by following
the links in the description box

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:43.360
below.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:46.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

